window.BLOG_POSTS = [{"id":"The Best Interface Is the One That Still Works","title":"The Best Interface Is the One That Still Works","date":"2026-07-21","preview":"Critical digital services should not be designed only for normal conditions. They should be designed for the moments when demand spikes, networks slow down, and users need answers quickly. Power outage systems are a good…","html":"<p>Critical digital services should not be designed only for normal conditions.</p>\n<p>They should be designed for the moments when demand spikes, networks slow down, and users need answers quickly.</p>\n<p>Power outage systems are a good example.</p>\n<p>During a major storm, most people do not need a complex interactive map first. They need a few reliable facts:</p>\n<p>Where is the outage?</p>\n<p>How many people are affected?</p>\n<p>Has a crew been assigned?</p>\n<p>When was the information last updated?</p>\n<p>What is the estimated restoration time?</p>\n<p>A better design could load a lightweight outage list by default, then offer the full map only when requested.</p>\n<p>That would reduce unnecessary data transfer and computation while also making the service easier to use on slow mobile connections.</p>\n<p>It could also improve trust by clearly showing whether information is live, delayed, or stale.</p>\n<p>This reflects a broader systems principle:</p>\n<p>The most impressive interface is not always the most useful one.</p>\n<p>For critical public services, resilience often comes from simplicity, progressive loading, clear status information, and keeping essential functions available when conditions are at their worst.</p>\n<p>Good systems are not just efficient when everything works.</p>\n<p>They remain useful when it doesn’t.</p>"},{"id":"Expectations Don’t Decide Outcomes","title":"Expectations Don’t Decide Outcomes","date":"2026-06-24","preview":"One of the most inspiring stories I’ve come across isn’t from business, technology, or leadership literature. It’s from the 1950 FIFA World Cup. Brazil entered the decisive match of the final round needing only a draw to…","html":"<p>One of the most inspiring stories I’ve come across isn’t from business, technology, or leadership literature.</p>\n<p>It’s from the 1950 FIFA World Cup.</p>\n<p>Brazil entered the decisive match of the final round needing only a draw to become world champions. The game was played in Rio de Janeiro in front of nearly 200,000 fans.</p>\n<p>Newspapers had already printed celebratory headlines.</p>\n<p>The country was preparing for a coronation, not a contest.</p>\n<p>Uruguay walked into one of the most intimidating environments in sports history.</p>\n<p>Fear was present.</p>\n<p>Doubt was present.</p>\n<p>The odds were stacked against them.</p>\n<p>Then Brazil took the lead early in the second half.</p>\n<p>Most teams would have accepted the script.</p>\n<p>Uruguay didn’t.</p>\n<p>They fought back, scored twice, and won 2-1 in what became known as the Maracanazo, one of the greatest upsets in sports history.</p>\n<p>The lesson isn’t that fear disappears when the moment gets big.</p>\n<p>It’s that outcomes aren’t decided by expectations.</p>\n<p>Brazil had the crowd.</p>\n<p>Brazil had the headlines.</p>\n<p>Brazil had the predictions.</p>\n<p>Uruguay still had to play the match.</p>\n<p>Sometimes the story everyone believes turns out to be wrong.</p>\n<p>The crowd, the headlines, and the predictions don’t play the game.</p>\n<p>You do.</p>"},{"id":"Robo-Advisors Can Democratize Investing, but Governance Matters","title":"Robo-Advisors Can Democratize Investing, but Governance Matters","date":"2026-06-23","preview":"I’ve been thinking about roboadvising in Canada, using Wealthsimple as an example, and it challenged some of my assumptions. My original view was that roboadvisors are wellsuited to routine portfolio management. They can…","html":"<p>I’ve been thinking about robo-advising in Canada, using <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/company/wealthsimple/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Wealthsimple</strong></a> as an example, and it challenged some of my assumptions.</p>\n<p>My original view was that robo-advisors are well-suited to routine portfolio management. They can provide scalable, lower-cost investing access based on a user’s goals, time horizon, financial situation, and risk tolerance.</p>\n<p>I still think that’s true.</p>\n<p>What changed is how I think about access.</p>\n<p>Traditional financial advice can be expensive, which means many households may not receive meaningful guidance at all. Robo-advisors can help lower that barrier by making basic investing and portfolio management more accessible.</p>\n<p>But there’s an important tension.</p>\n<p>The users who benefit most from lower-cost financial tools may also face the greatest information gap when trying to evaluate whether a recommendation is suitable, transparent, or aligned with their interests.</p>\n<p>That’s where governance matters.</p>\n<p>In low-stakes recommendation systems, a bad recommendation might mean ordering the wrong food or watching the wrong movie.</p>\n<p>In financial advice, a poor recommendation can affect someone’s long-term financial well-being, especially when financial literacy, income stability, debt, family obligations, or life circumstances make decisions more complex.</p>\n<p>So my view has evolved.</p>\n<p>I still see robo-advising as valuable for scalability and financial inclusion. But I increasingly see it as a complement to human advice rather than a full replacement.</p>\n<p>As AI systems take on more consequential decisions, suitability, transparency, privacy, and accountability become just as important as the technology itself.</p>"}];
